    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "This research project focuses on the potential to subvert gender norms within the digital sphere through individual identity creation and affective digital socialization by analyzing the babiniku (バ美肉) phenomenon. Babiniku is a term used to refer to self-proclaimed cis-gendered, heterosexual men who partake in a form of digital crossdressing through their online content creation. Rather than a desire to explore one’s gender or sexuality, babiniku are motivated by the social benefits of being perceived as feminine, as babiniku believe that certain human needs, such as support, comfort, and love, are only acceptable for those who are kawaii or “cute.” While on one hand, through openly acknowledging parts of their masculine identity babiniku are challenging the gendered power imbalances embedded within the notion of kawaii, at times, by playing into gendered stereotypes and often sexualizing the “cute” aspects of their character-persona, babiniku reinforce problematic gendered notions.\r\n\r\nIn this thesis, I will argue that the “subversiveness” of a babiniku, or how effective a babiniku is at challenging hegemonic gendered norms, depends on the ways a babiniku conceptualizes, portrays, and utilizes their identity to create intimate bonds with their fans. To do so, first, I will analyze gender identity gender-bending practices, particularly in the virtual realm and in relation to orientations influenced by the physical body. Secondly, I will examine authentic identity creation in the Virtual YouTuber (V-tuber) sphere, emphasizing that this authenticity which is felt by fans of V-tubers and V-tubers themselves is what gives babiniku the potential to redefine and create their own social norms. Thirdly, I will analyze Nora Cat and Nekomasu—two babiniku who represent opposing degrees of immersion that are possible with a digital avatar—in relation to gendered norms. Ultimately, through this research, I aim to illuminate the need to consider nuances within the relationship between gender-bending practices, identity, and normative structures.",
